I rewrote `merge_intervals` in `solution.py` so it follows the README. Both public tests pass. I also ran some extra checks by hand, and they all behaved as expected: the input list was left unchanged, empty input returned `[]`, and intervals that touch merged (`[1,2]` and `[2,3]` became `[1,3]`). Bad inputs raised the right errors: a bool, a float, the wrong length or a non-list container.

The function now:
- raises `TypeError` if `intervals` isn't a list;
- raises `ValueError` for any interval that isn't a two-item list or tuple of plain ints (bools don't count) with start no greater than end;
- copies the intervals, sorts them by start and merges any interval that begins at or before the end of the current merged one;
- returns a new list of `[start, end]` pairs and never changes the input.

DONE: Rewrote merge_intervals to validate input, sort by start, merge overlapping or touching intervals into a new list without changing the input; public tests pass.
